{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Therefore his name was called Edom.</b> Why did they not call him Edom immediately at birth, on account of 'the first came out red' — because this is nothing new under the sun, for many children are born red since their blood has not yet been absorbed, as in the case of Rabbi Nathan (Shabbat 134), and in the end the color changes and reverts to its normal appearance. Therefore those who begot him thought that perhaps this red appearance was incidental and not natural. But when he said, 'Stuff me now, I pray you, with this red, red stuff,' the question is hard: why did he not call them lentils by their name? Rather, he surely revealed his mind that he did not crave them for their own sake but only on account of their red appearance, and he thereby indicated that the temperament of his nature inclined strongly toward redness, because of the predominance of the red bile within him. Therefore he loved everything associated with his temperament — and that is everything red. Therefore his name was called Edom, for then it was truly known that his nature was red and he sits under the planet Mars, and on account of his being 'a man of blood.' And this is what he said, 'for I am exhausted' — Rashi explained: exhausted from killing, as in 'for my soul is exhausted from killing' (Jeremiah 4:31). And what did Esav tell them by this, that he is exhausted from killing? Rather, he surely gave his words a reason why he called the lentils 'red' — because at that time he was engaged with the tools of his craft in killing, according to his nature; therefore he asked to be fed from the food suited to his temperament. And in the Akedah [commentary] two reasons were given for the statement 'therefore his name was called Edom,' but what I have written fits more closely with the plain sense of the verse."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ובעקדה' refers to the Kli Yakar's own earlier commentary on the Akedah (Genesis 22); 'כלי אומנותו ברציחה' echoes the rabbinic tradition that Esav was engaged in murder when he came home famished; 'גברא אשד דמא' is Aramaic for 'a man who sheds blood.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:12.792725678Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}